== Differences from BIP 448 ==

The opcode numbers, template hashing, and BIP 340 verification algorithm are unchanged. This draft restricts the permitted CSFS message and adapts deployment to an RDTS validation context:
* CSFS MUST consume exactly the current input's 32-byte TEMPLATEHASH result. Arbitrary messages, including unrelated 32-byte hashes, are prohibited after activation, even with an empty signature or unknown key type. This deliberately narrows BIP 348/BIP 448 semantics.

* Unknown OP_SUCCESS operations are already rejected at consensus under RDTS. Allowing the three defined operations is therefore a consensus relaxation, unlike BIP 448's soft-fork deployment under ordinary Tapscript rules.
* RDTS stack-element and control-block limits remain in force. Annexes and Tapscript OP_IF and OP_NOTIF remain prohibited. Constructions using those features require changes or cannot run in this environment.
* Production activation is unspecified. The reference implementation is deployed only on an experimental signet and is available for regtest.

The CSFS message restriction is fixed consensus, not a configurable relay size limit. Header hashing does not replace SHA256 in the template hash, and existing transaction-signature rules do not automatically provide a replay domain for CSFS messages.

== Motivation ==

Bitcoin payment protocols use pre-signed transactions to constrain later spending. Collecting and storing those signatures can require repeated coordination among participants.

Transaction templates allow scripts to commit to the spending transaction. Signature verification over a template permits authorization that is not tied to a particular input outpoint. Access to the Taproot internal key avoids duplicating that key in the script.

These capabilities can support payment channels and shared-output protocols. They do not guarantee confirmation, eliminate fee requirements, or establish the security of a particular Ark or Lightning implementation.

BIP 448 specifies this operation set for an OP_SUCCESS-compatible validation context. Bitcoin's RDTS rules make unknown OP_SUCCESS rejection mandatory. Activation therefore needs an explicit exception for the three defined operations and cannot be described as a soft fork relative to the existing RDTS rules.

== Specification ==

=== Scope ===

The rules apply only to Taproot script-path spends with leaf version 0xc0, after activation. No new meaning is assigned in legacy Script or witness version 0.

{| class="wikitable"
! Byte !! Operation !! Definition
|-
| 0xcb || OP_INTERNALKEY || BIP 349
|-
| 0xcc || OP_CHECKSIGFROMSTACK || BIP 348, restricted below
|-
| 0xce || OP_TEMPLATEHASH || BIP 446
|}

An implementation MUST exclude these three bytes from unknown-OP_SUCCESS rejection when this proposal is active. All other RDTS restrictions remain in force. When inactive, the existing rules apply without these exceptions.

=== OP_TEMPLATEHASH ===

The operation pushes a 32-byte tagged SHA256 hash. Define:

  tag = SHA256("TemplateHash")
  template_hash = SHA256(tag || tag || message)

The message is the concatenation, in order, of:

* Transaction nVersion, serialized as 4 bytes little-endian.
* Transaction nLockTime, serialized as 4 bytes little-endian.
* SHA256 of all input nSequence values serialized in input order, each as 4 bytes little-endian.
* SHA256 of all transaction outputs serialized consecutively as CTxOut, without an additional vector-count prefix.
* annex_present, one byte, either 0 or 1.
* The index of the input being evaluated, as 4 bytes little-endian.
* If an annex is present, SHA256 of its CompactSize-prefixed serialization, as defined in BIP 341.

The hash does not commit to input outpoints, spent-output amounts, or spent-output scripts. The operation does not itself compare the result with a commitment; the surrounding script supplies that check or signature verification.

Implementations SHOULD reuse the BIP 341 precomputed sequence and output hashes. Header proof-of-work hashing does not change these SHA256 definitions.

The annex field is defined for compatibility with BIP 446. While RDTS is active, an annex is rejected before script execution, so the usable case has annex_present equal to zero.

=== OP_CHECKSIGFROMSTACK ===

The operation requires three stack items. From bottom to top, these are the signature, message, and public key. It removes these items.
Before signature or public-key-type handling, the message MUST be exactly 32 bytes and byte-for-byte equal to OP_TEMPLATEHASH for this transaction and this input index. Otherwise execution MUST fail, including for an empty signature. This is a value-equality rule: the digest may originate from the witness, a script constant or computation; opcode provenance is not tracked. Hashing unrelated data into 32 bytes does not satisfy the rule. Other inputs' template hashes do not satisfy it either.

A zero-length public key fails script execution. For a 32-byte public key, a nonempty signature MUST be exactly 64 bytes and pass BIP 340 verification of the supplied message. The message is passed directly to BIP 340 verification without an additional application-level hash. Verification failure terminates the script.

An empty signature produces false. A valid nonempty signature produces true. A nonzero public-key length other than 32 retains BIP 348's unknown-key-type behavior: no signature verification is performed, with the result determined by whether the signature is empty. Existing policy may reject unknown key types.

Every nonempty signature consumes 50 units of the Tapscript signature-validation budget, including unknown key types. A negative remaining budget fails execution. Empty signatures consume no signature-validation budget.

All existing stack, element-size, and script-execution limits apply. This operation does not inherit the transaction signature digest or its replay domain from ordinary CHECKSIG.

=== OP_INTERNALKEY ===

After validating the Taproot control block and its commitment to the spent output, the operation pushes the 32-byte x-only internal public key from control-block bytes 1 through 32.

It pushes the internal key, not the tweaked output key. The operation consumes no stack items and performs no signature check. Normal stack limits still apply.

=== RDTS interaction ===

The reference validation context retains:

* The 256-byte stack-element limit.
* The Taproot control-block limit of 257 bytes, allowing at most seven Merkle branch hashes.
* Rejection of Taproot annexes.
* Rejection of OP_IF and OP_NOTIF in Tapscript.
* Existing output restrictions and the other mandatory validation flags.

The only opcode-related RDTS exception introduced by this operation set is allowing the three defined operations to execute instead of treating them as prohibited unknown OP_SUCCESS operations. This document does not extend or permanently activate RDTS itself.

The experimental signet also retains an existing, bounded exception for its signed-block coinbase envelope. That signet-specific mechanism is not required by these opcodes.

=== Relay policy ===
Updated nodes enforce the template-only rule for mempool admission and local mining before its block activation. Previously mined blocks retain their historical rules. The consensus restriction cannot be disabled using a relay-policy option.

The 32-byte requirement is fixed consensus, not operator-configurable policy. The former maxcsfsmsgsize option is removed. No transaction-wide CSFS byte limit is added: a tested two-input Ark refund uses two distinct 32-byte template hashes. Historical blocks before activation retain their previous rules.

=== Activation ===

Production activation is unspecified. No production height, signaling mechanism, or deployment bit is assigned by this draft.
The experimental signet enforces template-only CSFS from block height 3250 inclusive. Earlier blocks retain the previous CSFS rules. Custom covenant regtest defaults to activation at height zero; the regtest-only testactivationheight=csfstemplate override can schedule another height or use -1 for historical-rule tests. There is no signet opt-out.

The experimental implementation enables the operation set with -btccovtest on custom signet or regtest. The option is rejected on other networks. This switch is a testing mechanism, not a production deployment proposal.

The existing experimental signet was upgraded without changing its signing challenge, network discriminator, or history. The first recorded INTERNALKEY demonstration spend is in block 3179. This is evidence of deployment, not a proposed consensus activation height.

== Rationale ==

The proposal preserves opcode numbers, template serialization, and the BIP 340 verification algorithm. Restricting CSFS messages to the transaction's template removes its general-purpose arbitrary-message authorization capability while preserving the tested covenant refresh and exit paths. General delegation and oracle constructions are not promised to remain compatible. Header hashing and transaction-template hashing serve different purposes; changing the template hash would unnecessarily break published vectors and signing implementations.

INTERNALKEY avoids embedding a duplicate internal key and allows the same script tree to be used with different internal keys in newly created outputs. It does not change an existing output's key or authorize spending by itself.

Keeping RDTS restrictions explicit prevents claiming support for constructions that require a prohibited annex, a deeper script tree, or a larger stack element.

An alternative is to adopt BIP 448 without retaining the relevant RDTS restrictions. That would have a broader consensus scope. This draft limits its change to the operation set and its required opcode exceptions.

== Security considerations ==
Template-only CSFS is not a guarantee against arbitrary data elsewhere in a transaction. Keys, outputs and other script paths still require their own analysis. The rule restricts messages, not the existing unknown-key-type upgrade semantics. Tightening rules can make outstanding outputs relying on arbitrary-message CSFS unspendable; network upgrades must account for such outputs before activation.

Template commitments omit input outpoints and amounts. Reusing signing keys or messages across outputs or networks can therefore authorize unintended spends. Applications must define and validate their signing domains and funding assumptions.

An overfunded template may send excess value to fees. An underfunded template may be unspendable unless its committed input structure permits additional funding. Application recovery and fee-bumping designs require separate review.

A usable Taproot key path can bypass script-path conditions. The presence of a template script does not establish that every spend must follow that template.

These rules do not guarantee timely confirmation, safe unilateral exit, or resistance to every fee-pinning strategy. Relay-policy assumptions must be kept separate from consensus guarantees.

Implementations must check stack bounds, signature-budget accounting, template serialization, and the validated origin of the internal key. Cached transaction data must correspond to the transaction being evaluated.

== Backward compatibility ==
The template-only addition is a tightening relative to the earlier unrestricted-CSFS experimental client: old clients may accept blocks that upgraded clients reject. Participants must upgrade to enforce it. The initial addition of the three operations under RDTS remains the separate consensus relaxation described below.

Under ordinary BIP 342 rules, assigning meaning to OP_SUCCESS operations tightens validity and can be deployed as a soft fork. Under RDTS, those unknown operations are already rejected at consensus. Allowing them is a relaxation of the existing rules and requires a coordinated consensus upgrade.

An old signet client rejects a block containing an INTERNALKEY spend. Users must upgrade before validating such blocks. A client that already cached the demonstration block as invalid must upgrade and then reconsider that block, or rebuild its block index with the updated client.

Existing signature algorithms, proof-of-work rules, addresses, and transaction serialization are unchanged by this proposal. Existing spends that do not use these operations retain their previous behavior.

== Reference implementation and tests ==

The implementation builds on [https://github.com/bitcoinknots/bitcoin Bitcoin Knots], which provides the RDTS and signature-validation context for this adaptation. The experimental changes are maintained separately. This reference does not imply acceptance or endorsement by Bitcoin Knots maintainers.

The reference implementation is:
https://github.com/connorslab/paperclip-bitcoin-signet/commit/8b0c84ea587189af4bb3a97faea21845b77fb83d

Relevant files include src/script/interpreter.cpp, src/script/interpreter.h, src/script/script.h, src/test/script_tests.cpp, and test/functional/feature_bitcoin_covenants.py.

The reference tests include BIP 446 basic vectors, BIP 340 vectors through CSFS, internal-key extraction, rejection outside Tapscript, combined three-opcode spends under two keys, altered-output rejection, mining, and restart checks. Native validation passed 19 selected unit suites and covenant, signed-signet, historical-CSFS, template-only activation, RDTS, and unified-sighash functional tests. Both single-balance experimental Ark refresh and ASP/watchman lifecycle harnesses passed against the deployment binary. Tests cover wrong-length and unrelated messages, empty signatures, historical block acceptance, rejection at activation, restart, and independent exit. These results do not establish compatibility for all Ark constructions.

The full BIP 446 script-assets corpus has not been imported. Exhaustive fuzzing and independent consensus review remain outstanding. Passing these tests is not a protocol-security audit.

Deployment evidence is recorded in [[deployment.md]].

